--  Running another program: a seventh concept beside the six, outside
--  `world`, through which a program starts a child by its path, with the
--  arguments and the environment it gives it, connects each of the child's
--  standard streams, reads what the child writes and waits for it to end.

import core/text

--- A child's standard stream is the parent's own: it reads or writes
--- where the parent does.
public inherited: atom
--- A child's standard stream is connected to nothing: its input reads at
--- once to its end, and what it writes is thrown away.
public discarded: atom
--- A child's standard stream is a pipe the parent holds the other end of:
--- the parent writes the child's input, or reads its output or errors.
public piped: atom
--- How one of a child's standard streams is connected: `inherited`,
--- `discarded` or `piped`.
public stream_choice: type = inherited | discarded | piped

--- How each of a child's three standard streams is connected.
public child_streams: type = struct
    input: stream_choice
    output: stream_choice
    errors: stream_choice
end child_streams

--- Every stream the parent's own: the child reads and writes where the
--- parent does.
public inheriting: child_streams = (input: inherited, output: inherited,
                                    errors: inherited)

--- Every stream connected to nothing: a child run for how it ends.
public discarding: child_streams = (input: discarded, output: discarded,
                                    errors: discarded)

--- The child's output and errors piped to the parent, which reads them with
--- `read_child`, and its input discarded.
public capturing: child_streams = (input: discarded, output: piped,
                                   errors: piped)

--- A started child: a value its caller keeps and edits, passing it `inout`
--- to `write_child`, `close_input` and `read_child`, and `sink` to `wait`,
--- which consumes it.  Its fields are the provider's: `handle` names the
--- child, and `input`, `output` and `errors` the parent's ends of its piped
--- streams, each open while its `*_open` flag says so, which it is only
--- for a piped stream.  The child owns its pipes: `close_input` closes its
--- input, `read_child` closes an output it has read to the end, and `wait`
--- closes the rest.  A provider whose own files they are, as the hosted
--- one's are its descriptors, may read and write them through `reader` and
--- `writer` as well, a line reader over a child's output among them; never
--- close them through `files`.  A child that is never waited for keeps
--- what its provider holds for it until the process ends: there are no
--- destructors.
public child: type = struct
    handle: usize
    input: file
    output: file
    errors: file
    input_open: bool
    output_open: bool
    errors_open: bool
end child

--- What `read_child` read: `count` bytes, from the child's errors when
--- `errors` is true and from its output when it is not, or nothing and
--- `finished`, once every piped output stream of the child has reached its
--- end.
public child_read: type = struct
    count: usize
    errors: bool
    finished: bool
end child_read

--- How a child ended: it `exited` with the code its program returned, or
--- it was `signalled`, ended from outside before it returned one.  What
--- ended it is the system's detail, which its provider keeps; on POSIX it
--- is a signal.  A POSIX code is the low eight bits of the program's
--- value, 0 to 255.
public ending: type = struct
    how: variant
        exited: (code: i32) |
        signalled
    end how
end ending

--- Starting programs and waiting for them.  A program is named by its
--- path, a host string, never looked up on a search path nor given to a
--- shell; a child is given exactly the arguments and the environment its
--- parent builds, and inherits no other open file.  Each of its three
--- standard streams is inherited, discarded or piped (`child_streams`).
---
--- A child that writes more than a pipe holds waits for its parent to read
--- it, so read a piped output while the child runs, and read output and
--- errors piped together through `read_child`, which reads whichever has
--- something: reading one to its end first can wait forever while the
--- child waits to write the other.  Close a piped input before waiting for
--- a child that reads it to its end.
public programs: type = concept (provider: type)
    --- Start the program at `path` with `arguments`, which it sees after
    --- its own name, and exactly the variables of `environment`.  A
    --- missing program is `not_found`, one the caller may not run
    --- `no_access`; on any failure no child was started.
    spawn: (self: provider, path: text.host_string,
            arguments: []text.host_string, environment: []variable,
            streams: child_streams)
           -> (started: child) ! not_found | no_access | io_failed
    --- Write all of `bytes` to the child's piped input, waiting while its
    --- pipe is full.
    write_child: (self: provider, inout started: child, bytes: []u8)
                 -> none ! io_failed
    --- Close the child's piped input, so that it reads to its end.
    close_input: (self: provider, inout started: child) -> none ! io_failed
    --- Read what the child wrote next to whichever of its piped output and
    --- errors has something, into `into`.
    read_child: (self: provider, inout started: child, into: []mut u8)
                -> (taken: child_read) ! io_failed
    --- Wait for the child to end, close every pipe it still has, and
    --- report how it ended.  The child is consumed even when this fails.
    wait: (self: provider, sink started: child)
          -> (ended: ending) ! io_failed
end programs

--- Start the program at `path` through `programs` evidence, with
--- `arguments` after its name and exactly the variables of `environment`,
--- its standard streams connected as `streams` says.  Wait for every child
--- started.
public spawn: (provider: type is programs, host: provider,
               path: text.host_string, arguments: []text.host_string,
               environment: []variable, streams: child_streams)
              -> (started: child) ! not_found | no_access | io_failed =
    started = try provider.spawn(host, path, arguments, environment, streams)
end spawn

--- Write all of `bytes` to a child's piped input.  A child that is not
--- reading waits its parent here once its pipe is full, so write a large
--- input only to a child that reads it as it comes.  Writing to an input
--- that is not open is `io_failed`.
public write_child: (provider: type is programs, host: provider,
                     inout started: child, bytes: []u8) -> none ! io_failed =
    try provider.write_child(host, started, bytes)
end write_child

--- Close a child's piped input, which it then reads to its end.  Closing
--- an input that is not open is `io_failed`.
public close_input: (provider: type is programs, host: provider,
                     inout started: child) -> none ! io_failed =
    try provider.close_input(host, started)
end close_input

--- Read the next bytes a child wrote to whichever of its piped output and
--- errors has something, waiting until one has; zero bytes and `finished`
--- once both have ended.
public read_child: (provider: type is programs, host: provider,
                    inout started: child, into: []mut u8)
                   -> (taken: child_read) ! io_failed =
    taken = try provider.read_child(host, started, into)
end read_child

--- Wait for a child to end and report how it did.  The child is consumed,
--- with every pipe it still had, even when this reports `io_failed`.
public wait: (provider: type is programs, host: provider,
              sink started: child) -> (ended: ending) ! io_failed =
    ended = try provider.wait(host, started)
end wait
